 "module": "m53586",
 "chapter": "3",
 "title": "Derivatives of Exponential and Logarithmic Functions",
 "short": "Exponential and Logarithmic Derivatives",
 "lead": "Assuming only that bˣ has a <span data-concept=\"derivative\">derivative</span> at 0, the derivative of every <span data-concept=\"exponential-function\">exponential function</span> follows, and the number e is the base whose tangent at 0 has <span data-concept=\"slope\">slope</span> 1. The derivatives of the <span data-concept=\"natural-logarithm\">natural logarithm</span> and of the general exponential and logarithmic functions come next, then <span data-concept=\"logarithmic-differentiation\">logarithmic differentiation</span> for functions such as xˣ.",
 "objectives": [
  "Find the derivative of exponential functions.",
  "Find the derivative of logarithmic functions.",
  "Use logarithmic differentiation to determine the derivative of a function."
 ],
 "summary_html": "<ul><li>On the basis of the assumption that the <span data-concept=\"exponential-function\">exponential function</span> $y=b^{x},b\\gt 0$ is continuous everywhere and differentiable at 0, this <span data-concept=\"function\">function</span> is differentiable everywhere and there is a formula for its <span data-concept=\"derivative\">derivative</span>.</li><li>We can use a formula to find the <span data-concept=\"derivative\">derivative</span> of $y=\\ln x,$ and the relationship ${\\log }_{b}x=\\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln b}$ allows us to extend our differentiation formulas to include logarithms with arbitrary bases.</li><li><span data-concept=\"logarithmic-differentiation\">Logarithmic differentiation</span> allows us to differentiate <span data-concept=\"function\">functions</span> of the form $y=g{(x)}^{\\kf(x)}$ or very complex <span data-concept=\"function\">functions</span> by taking the <span data-concept=\"natural-logarithm\">natural logarithm</span> of both sides and exploiting the <span data-concept=\"properties-of-logarithms\">properties of logarithms</span> before differentiating.</li></ul>",
