   "cite": "exponential",
   "prompt": "<p>For the following exercises, use the population of New York City from 1790 to 1860, given in the following table.</p><div class=\"book-table\" id=\"fs-id1169738227097\"><div class=\"tcap\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">Table 3.8</span><span>New York City Population Over Time</span></div><table><thead><tr><th>Years since 1790</th><th>Population</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>33,131</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>60,515</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>96,373</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>123,706</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>202,300</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td>312,710</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>515,547</td></tr><tr><td>70</td><td>813,669</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>[T]</strong> Using the tables of first and second derivatives and the best fit, answer the following questions:</p><p>a. Will the model be accurate in predicting the future population of New York City? Why or why not?</p><p>b. Estimate the population in 2010. Was the prediction correct from a.?</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "solution": "a. No. The best fit $p=35741{(1.045)}^{t}$ is exponential, and the tables show its first and second derivatives growing larger every decade, so the model has the population growing ever faster without bound. A city’s population is limited by space and resources and cannot keep growing faster and faster, so the model will not predict the future population accurately for long. b. The year 2010 is $t=220$ years after 1790: $p=35741{(1.045)}^{220}\\approx 5.7\\times {10}^{8},$ about 574 million people. The population of New York City in 2010 was about 8.2 million, so the prediction is far too large, as part a. expects.",
    "generated_by": "ai"
   }
